  "worked_solution": "For the given matrix $C$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3},$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $6$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{6}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{5}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $C$ must be a $25\\times 25$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{5}$ equals $10,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{5}=25-10=15.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{5}$ of dimension $15$ in ${R}^{25}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{6}$ equals $8,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{6}=25-8=17.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{6}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{6}$ of dimension $17$ in ${R}^{25}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{5}$ is a subspace of dimension $15$ in $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{6}$, which is a subspace of dimension $17,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{6}$ but do not lie in $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{6}-\\text{nullity}(C-\\sqrt{3}I)^{5}=17-15=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $6$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}.$",
